---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "be well by kelly protein powder",
      "be well by kelly grass fed beef protein powder",
      "be well protein powder",
      "be well by kelly protein review",
      "kelly leveque protein powder",
      "be well by kelly vanilla protein",
      "be well by kelly unflavored protein",
      "beef protein isolate review"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Beef Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-beef-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Be Well by Kelly Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Kelly LeVeque's grass-fed beef protein has one of the shortest ingredient lists in the category and one of the highest prices. Here is what is in each bag, how much of it is collagen, and what 25 g of protein really costs.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#who-makes-it">Who Is Kelly LeVeque</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Which Be Well to Buy</a>
      <a href="#label">The Label, Flavor by Flavor</a>
      <a href="#collagen">The Collagen Question</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#sweetener-testing">Monk Fruit, Allergens, Testing</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#who-skip">Who It Suits, Who Should Skip</a>
      <a href="#rivals">Be Well vs the Rivals</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Be Well by Kelly makes a clean, dairy-free beef protein with a very short label: one ingredient in Unflavored, three in Vanilla, Chocolate and Chai, sweetened only with monk fruit. Per the brand's labels at the time of writing, a scoop gives 23 to 24 g of protein in about 100 calories. Two things the front of the bag does not tell you: the brand's own FAQ says an average scoop holds about 16 g of collagen, and at $64.99 for 30 servings you pay about $2.35 per 25 g of protein. Buy it if you need dairy-free animal protein and value the short label. Do not buy it as a whey substitute for building muscle on a budget.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Be Well by Kelly is a good product with a narrow audience. Its grass-fed beef protein has one of the shortest labels you will find in the category: Unflavored is a single ingredient, and Vanilla, Chocolate and Chai add only an organic flavoring and organic monk fruit. There are no gums, no fillers, no stevia, no sucralose and no added sugar. Per the brand, a scoop gives 23 to 24 g of protein, and the Chai panel we read lists 100 calories, 0.5 g of fat and 1 g of carbohydrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            What you are actually buying needs a closer look. Like most beef isolates, this one is rich in connective-tissue protein, and the brand says so openly: its FAQ states that more than half of the amino acids are collagen amino acids and that testing showed an average of about 16 g of collagen per scoop. That is useful protein, but it is not the muscle-building equal of 23 g of whey. You get less leucine per gram, so a single scoop is a weaker post-workout stimulus than the number on the bag suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is the price. At $64.99 for 30 servings on Amazon and the brand site at the time of writing, Be Well costs about $2.35 per 25 g of protein for Vanilla, Unflavored and Chai, and about $2.26 for Chocolate. Mainstream beef isolate such as Carnivor runs about $1.11, and a good whey isolate usually lands between $1 and $1.40. You are paying roughly double for the short label, the grass-fed sourcing and the Kelly LeVeque name.
          </p>

          <p>
            In short: buy Be Well if you need a dairy-free animal protein, you avoid artificial sweeteners and stevia, and the price does not bother you. Start with Vanilla. Skip it if you tolerate dairy, if you want the most muscle per dollar, or if you need a certified product for drug-tested sport. Our <a href="/best-beef-protein-powder/">beef protein powder rankings</a> cover the cheaper and better-documented options.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">The cleanest beef protein label we have read, at about $2.35 per 25 g.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Buy Vanilla if you are dairy-free and want monk fruit rather than stevia or sucralose. If you just need dairy-free protein grams, Carnivor delivers the same 23 g for less than half the price.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Is Kelly LeVeque, and What Is Be Well?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Be Well by Kelly is the supplement and food brand of Kelly LeVeque, a Los Angeles-based holistic nutritionist and celebrity health coach. She is best known for the Fab Four, a simple framework built around eating protein, fat, fiber and greens at every meal, and for her books Body Love (2017) and Body Love Every Day (2019). Press coverage has named clients including Jessica Alba, Jennifer Garner, Chelsea Handler and Emmy Rossum, though those details come from interviews rather than anything we could independently confirm.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein powders exist to serve that framework. Kelly's signature recipe is the Fab Four smoothie: one serving of protein, a tablespoon or two of fat such as nut butter or avocado, a tablespoon or two of fiber such as chia or flax, unlimited greens and a liquid. The brand's bags print that recipe on the back. That context explains the product choices: a low-carb, low-sugar protein designed to be blended with fat and fiber, not shaken with water after a workout.
          </p>

          <p>
            The current range at the time of writing includes beef protein in Unflavored, Vanilla, Chocolate, Chai, Pumpkin Spice and Chocolate Mint, plus a vegan line made from chocho, a type of Andean lupin bean, in Unflavored, Vanilla and Chocolate. The brand also sells essential amino acids, creatine, magnesium, an omega supplement and kitchen gear. Beef bags are $64.99 for 30 servings on the brand site and Amazon, with 10 percent off orders over $150, 15 percent off over $200 and an extra 5 percent on subscription, per the brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            The beef is described as Swedish grass-fed on the brand site and as grass-fed or grass-finished in Amazon titles. The brand's FAQ adds that the isolate is sourced worldwide while manufacturing and fulfillment happen in the US, and the Chai bag names Primarch Manufacturing in Vista, California as the manufacturer. We have not tasted or tested Be Well ourselves. Taste and mixing notes here are buyer feedback, and figures come from the brand's labels, FAQ and Amazon listings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Be Well by Kelly Protein to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            All four Be Well beef proteins below are sold on Amazon US in 30-serving bags at $64.99 at the time of writing, the same list price as the brand site. They share the same grass-fed beef isolate base and differ only in flavoring. The fifth card is a much cheaper beef isolate for readers who mainly want dairy-free protein grams. Label figures are per the brand or the Amazon listing at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Label, Flavor by Flavor
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Chai bag is the panel we could read in full on the brand site at the time of writing, and the other flavors share the same beef isolate base. One scoop of about 25 g gives the figures below. Pumpkin Spice, a seasonal flavor, uses a larger 29 g scoop with 110 calories and about 4 g of carbohydrate because it includes real pumpkin powder, per its bag.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Flavor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ingredients</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Bag size</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Unflavored</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grass-fed beef isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">720 g, 30 servings</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vanilla</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Beef isolate, organic vanilla bean, organic monk fruit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">732 g, 30 servings</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chocolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Beef isolate, organic cacao, organic monk fruit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">849 g, 30 servings</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chai</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Beef isolate, organic chai spice blend, organic monk fruit extract</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g scoop, 30 servings</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pumpkin Spice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Beef isolate, organic pumpkin, organic spice blend, organic monk fruit extract</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 29 g scoop, 30 servings</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The Chai panel lists 100 calories, 0.5 g of fat with no saturated fat, 150 mg of sodium, 1 g of carbohydrate with less than 1 g of fiber, 0 g of sugar and 23 g of protein, plus 1.3 mg of iron, 15 mg of calcium and 80 mg of potassium. That is a very lean profile: about 92 percent of the calories come from protein. Vanilla and Unflavored are listed at 0 g of carbohydrate; Chocolate at 1 to 2 g, depending on whether you read the Amazon title or the brand page.
          </p>

          <p>
            What is missing is as notable as what is there. There are no gums such as xanthan or guar, no lecithin, no natural flavors and no salt added beyond what is in the protein. That is why the label is so short, and it is also why the powder clumps, which we cover in the taste section. The brand says the isolate is produced without hexane, a solvent sometimes used in protein processing, and that it is free of dairy, soy, gluten and added sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            The amino acid claims need a careful read. The brand says the protein contains all nine essential amino acids, including the three BCAAs, which is true of beef isolate. Being complete is a low bar, though: it means every essential amino acid is present, not that each is present in the amounts that matter most for muscle. The brand does not publish a full amino acid panel or a leucine figure, so the best evidence we have about the profile is its own collagen statement, covered next.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="collagen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Collagen Question: What 23 g of Beef Protein Really Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most beef protein isolates are made from hydrolyzed beef tissue that is rich in connective tissue, so a large share of the protein is collagen. Collagen is unusual: about a third of its amino acids are glycine, with lots of proline and hydroxyproline, and it is low in leucine and essentially missing tryptophan. Be Well is more open about this than most brands. Its FAQ says more than half the amino acids are collagen amino acids and that testing showed an average of 16 g of collagen per scoop, with some variation from lot to lot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the numbers and roughly 70 percent of a 23 g scoop is collagen. The brand presents that as a plus, and for some buyers it is: the FAQ suggests the protein can replace a separate collagen supplement, which could save you money if you already buy one. It also means the shake behaves more like a collagen drink with some muscle protein added than like a steak in a bag. If skin, hair and joint support is your main reason for buying, our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">collagen protein powder guide</a> compares dedicated collagen products that cost far less per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            For muscle, the key number is leucine, the amino acid that most strongly switches on muscle protein synthesis. Whey protein is roughly 10 to 11 percent leucine, so 23 g of whey supplies about 2.3 to 2.5 g. Collagen is roughly 3 percent leucine. Be Well does not publish its leucine content, but using typical values for collagen and beef muscle protein, our rough estimate for a scoop with 16 g of collagen is about 1 g of leucine. Treat that as an estimate, not a label figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            This does not make Be Well useless for training. Collagen-rich protein still counts toward your daily total, and small trials comparing beef protein isolate with whey found similar lean mass gains when people took about 46 g a day alongside resistance training. The practical fix is to dose higher, use two scoops after training or pair one scoop with a leucine-rich food such as eggs, fish or meat. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> shows how to set your daily target.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">DOSE IT LIKE THIS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Count about a third of each scoop as muscle protein.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">One scoop is a fine smoothie top-up inside a meal. As a stand-alone post-workout shake, use two scoops, or add a leucine-rich food, to get close to what one scoop of whey would deliver.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every Be Well beef bag is $64.99 for 30 servings at the time of writing, on both Amazon and the brand site. That is about $2.17 per scoop. At 23 g of protein per scoop for Vanilla, Unflavored and Chai, each bag holds 690 g of protein, which works out at about $2.35 per 25 g. Chocolate's 24 g scoop brings it down to about $2.26. Prices change, so treat these as the price we saw rather than a promise.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers (at $64.99)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
