                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">23-24 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">30</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Servings per bag</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$2.17</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2.26-2.35</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g of protein</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Bundles help a little. The brand site takes 10 percent off orders over $150 and 15 percent off orders over $200, plus 5 percent on subscription. Three bags at $194.97 become about $175, or roughly $2.12 per 25 g; four bags with the 15 percent discount come to about $2 per 25 g. Amazon also sells two-bag bundles at $123.48 at the time of writing, which is a small saving. Single-serve sample packs are $5 each on the brand site, which is a costly way to buy protein but a cheap way to test a flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            For context, mainstream beef isolate is far cheaper. MuscleMeds Carnivor's 56-serving tub was about $57 when we checked, around $1.11 per 25 g. Equip's unflavored Prime Protein was $67.99 for 30 servings of about 21 to 22 g, roughly $2.60 to $2.70 per 25 g, so Be Well is actually a little cheaper than its closest clean-label rival. A good whey isolate typically costs $1 to $1.40 per 25 g, and budget whey, like the tubs in our <a href="/kirkland-protein-powder-review/">Kirkland protein review</a>, can go lower.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand's own FAQ argues the price is fair because many powders serve only 10 to 15 g of protein. That comparison does not hold up against the category: most mainstream powders serve 20 to 30 g. The fair way to judge Be Well is per gram, and per gram it is a premium product. Whether that premium is worth it depends on how much you value grass-fed beef, monk fruit and a one-to-three-ingredient label over price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sweetener-testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Monk Fruit, Allergens and Third-Party Testing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every flavored Be Well protein is sweetened with organic monk fruit and nothing else. There is no stevia, sucralose, erythritol or other sugar alcohol, which makes it one of the few beef proteins that suits people who avoid all three. The brand says it chose monk fruit over stevia because stevia can taste bitter or like licorice and belongs to the ragweed family. If you are hunting for a powder without stevia, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-stevia/">stevia-free protein guide</a> lists other options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Monk fruit is not for everyone. The brand acknowledges in its FAQ that a small number of people get headaches or stomach upset from alternative sweeteners, and the most common taste complaint in buyer reviews is that Vanilla and Chocolate are sweeter than expected. The brand's own suggestion is to mix flavored and Unflavored powder half and half, which also makes a bag last longer for no extra cost. Unflavored contains no sweetener at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            On allergens, the protein itself contains no dairy, soy, gluten or egg, but the bag states it is manufactured in a facility that handles milk, soy, egg, tree nut, fish and wheat. That matters if you have a serious milk allergy rather than lactose intolerance, so check with your allergist before relying on it. The beef protein is not kosher, per the brand. The vegan chocho line is made from a lupin bean, and lupin is a recognized allergen that can cross-react in people with peanut allergy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Testing is the weak spot. We could not find published heavy metal results, batch certificates of analysis, NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or a Clean Label Project certification for Be Well on the brand site or the certifiers' listings we checked. One third-party shopping guide says the brand undergoes third-party testing, but without published results there is nothing to verify. Equip and Transparent Labs say more about their testing, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">heavy metals guide</a> explains what to look for. If you buy Chocolate, rotating with Vanilla is a sensible hedge, because cocoa is the main source of lead in protein powders.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Taste and Mixing: What Buyers Report
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyer feedback on the brand site is mostly positive, with Unflavored averaging about 4.5 stars across more than 120 reviews at the time of writing. The positive reviews focus on how filling it is, the short ingredient list and how easily it disappears into a smoothie. Several buyers say they use Unflavored daily in coffee or recipes specifically to avoid the taste of sweeteners.
          </p>

          <p>
            The recurring complaint is texture. Because there are no gums, lecithin or other emulsifiers, the collagen-rich powder clumps and sticks to the sides of a blender. The brand says this openly and recommends adding liquid to a high-powered blender first and the powder second. Buyers report that a hand frother or shaker bottle leaves clumps, and that it does not mix well into hot drinks. The bag's own instruction is blunt: blend, don't shake. If you only own a shaker cup, this is the wrong powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            On flavor, buyers describe Unflavored as genuinely neutral with a faint bone broth note that some notice in plain milk and few notice in a smoothie. Vanilla gets the broadest praise. Chocolate draws comments that it is mild rather than rich, and the brand suggests adding cacao nibs or unsweetened cocoa. Chai and Pumpkin Spice split opinion on how strong the spice is, which is normal for spiced powders and worth testing with a $5 sample pack first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature matters too. The brand's manufacturer advises keeping the powder below about 120 degrees F when mixing, so the right way to make a protein latte or hot chocolate is to blend the powder into warm milk first and then add coffee. For more ways to use a protein like this, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">protein powder for smoothies guide</a> covers blending tips and better-mixing alternatives. Unopened bags expire two years from the manufacturing date, per the brand; our guide to <a href="/does-protein-powder-expire/">whether protein powder expires</a> explains what happens after that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Be Well Suits, and Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Be Well makes the most sense for people who need an animal protein without dairy: those with lactose intolerance that lactose-free whey still bothers, people who follow paleo or carnivore-style diets that exclude dairy and legumes, and people who dislike the texture and taste of pea and rice blends. It is also a good fit if you avoid stevia, sucralose and sugar alcohols, because monk fruit is the only sweetener in the range. Our <a href="/best-dairy-free-protein-powder/">dairy-free protein powder rankings</a> compare it with egg and plant options.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also suits Fab Four followers, which is who it was designed for. One scoop in a smoothie with fat, fiber and greens gives you a meal with plenty of protein and almost no sugar. For keto and paleo eaters, the near-zero carbohydrate count and clean label are genuine advantages; our <a href="/best-paleo-protein-powder/">paleo protein powder guide</a> ranks other dairy-free and grain-free powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you tolerate dairy and mainly want to build muscle. A whey isolate gives you more leucine per gram at about half the price, so the same budget buys you roughly twice as much effective protein. Skip it too if you compete in a drug-tested sport, because we could not find a third-party sport certification, and if you only own a shaker bottle, because buyers consistently report clumping without a blender.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some people should check with a professional first. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant or breastfeeding, or have a severe food allergy, talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement; the brand itself advises consulting a doctor during pregnancy and a pediatrician before giving it to young children. The facility allergen statement matters for severe milk allergy, and the vegan chocho line is not a safe swap for anyone with a lupin or peanut allergy. This review is not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

