              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The brand says about 16 g of each scoop is collagen, which is low in leucine. One scoop is a weaker muscle-building dose than 23 g of whey. Use two scoops after training, or pair one with eggs, fish or meat, if muscle gain is the goal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaking it in a shaker bottle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                There are no gums or emulsifiers, so Be Well clumps and sticks without a blender. The brand's own instruction is to blend, not shake. Add your liquid to the blender first, then the powder, and keep drinks below about 120 degrees F.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming dairy-free means milk-allergy safe</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The protein contains no dairy, but the bag says it is made in a facility that handles milk, soy, egg, tree nut, fish and wheat. That is fine for most lactose intolerance; with a serious milk allergy, ask your allergist before relying on it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying full price for single bags</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At $64.99 a bag, Be Well is about $2.35 per 25 g. The brand site takes 10 to 15 percent off larger orders and 5 percent on subscription, which brings it nearer $2. If you will use it every day, buy in bulk or choose a cheaper beef isolate.
              </p>
